<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rim-Sin II</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rim-Sin II was a pretender who led a widespread rebellion of southern Mesopotamian cities against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> shortly after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>. According to the Babylonian Empire profile, the revolt spread across 26 southern cities and was crushed by Hammurabi's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a>. The victory came at great cost, as the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a> were pulled down and these cities ceased to function as administrative centres.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rim-sin-ii.jpg" alt="Rim-Sin II" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hammurabi%27s_Babylonia_1.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rim-Sin II, also written Rīm-Sîn II, ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> and was the last of the Dynasty of Larsa. He was a contemporary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and his reign is now thought to have lasted less than two years. He is mentioned as a son of one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warad-sin">Warad-Sin</a>, but it is unclear whether this was the earlier ruler of Larsa of that name, and it is not even clear that he came from Larsa. One of his letters suggests that his family came from Kesh, which he calls the city of the one who bore him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His name was sometimes preceded by the dingir, the sign for a god, though there is no record of his being deified. In a letter he wrote that the great gods had set the foundation of his throne firmly in Kesh to bring light to Emutbalum and gather its scattered people. Several of his year names survive. They record the laying of foundations for the Emudkur temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> and his raising to kingship over the land by the goddess Ninmah in the Kesh temple; one version adds that the evil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a> had not retreated from the land.</p><p>The rebellion began in the eighth regnal year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a>. The rebellious cities included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazallu">Kazallu</a>, Kutalla and Bad-tibira, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> in the north, and among Rim-Sin's fellow rebels were Ilūni of Eshnunna, Rim-Šara of Umma and Rîm-Anum of Uruk. Kassite troops served in his forces. For a time he controlled Nippur and Ur: documents at Nippur were dated by his reign after Samsu-iluna's year 8 and by Samsu-iluna's again from his year 11.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>About 18 months after it began, the rebellion ended for Rim-Sin II with his death and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a>. In Samsu-iluna's own account, written from his side, the year was not half over when he killed Rim-Sin, the suborner of Emutbalum, and heaped up a burial mound over him in the land of Kiš; he also captured Iluni of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> and had his throat cut. The wider revolt went on until Samsu-iluna's fourteenth year. The conflict is mentioned in the much later, fragmentary Chronicle of Ancient Kings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rim-Sîn II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>About a decade after Hammurabi's death in 1750 BCE, his son Samsu-iluna faced a widespread revolt across the south, led by a pretender styling himself <b>Rim-Sin II</b> and joined, according to Samsu-iluna's own inscriptions, by 26 rebel kings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
